WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:02.936
In this lesson, you will have everything you need

2
00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:06.840
to assemble a fully stateful Memory Aware Agent.

3
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.242
You will implement a startup routine

4
00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:12.712
that loads prior context from long-term memory,

5
00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:14.814
build a recursive reason loop

6
00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:17.117
and intermediate memory checkpoints

7
00:00:17.117 --> 00:00:20.320
and creates an agent that persists across sessions

8
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.322
and improve over time.

9
00:00:22.756 --> 00:00:25.258
Let's have some fun. In this lesson

10
00:00:25.258 --> 00:00:27.560
we're going to touch on memory-aware agents.

11
00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:30.563
We're going to cover, specifically, what the agent loop is.

12
00:00:31.064 --> 00:00:33.299
We'll look at memory operations that are

13
00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:35.568
in and outside of the agent loop.

14
00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:38.271
We'll also get an overview of agent harness

15
00:00:38.605 --> 00:00:42.409
And generally, we're going to implement a memory-aware agent.

16
00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:47.080
The Agent Loop is this cyclical iterative environment

17
00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.483
in which an LLM executes for a specific amount of time.

18
00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.553
it will start with assembling the context,

19
00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:56.056
which we've been talking about throughout this course.

20
00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:59.659
Then we will pass the context that we've assembled

21
00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:03.630
into an LLM to enable the LLM to reason

22
00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.533
and decide on the information it's received.

23
00:01:07.434 --> 00:01:09.235
the LLM would then take an action.

24
00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:11.838
The action could be to respond, to call tools,

25
00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:14.541
or to ask the user for further input.

26
00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:17.844
But generally, this is what we refer to as the agent loop.

27
00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:20.880
where we assemble context,

28
00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.583
invoke the LLM and act on the response from the LLM.

29
00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:25.885
It's cyclical and iterative,

30
00:01:25.885 --> 00:01:27.720
which means this loop can be

31
00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.889
executed for a specific amount of time.

32
00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:34.160
termination of a loop is usually determined by a stop condition.

33
00:01:34.561 --> 00:01:36.963
This could be a final answer from the LLM

34
00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:38.932
when the goal is completed or

35
00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:40.867
when we have an error or timeout

36
00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:42.869
on the loop execution.

37
00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:46.506
The Agent Loop starts when the user provides context

38
00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:48.808
or input into the Agent Loop

39
00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:52.912
and begins the assembly of context process.

40
00:01:53.313 --> 00:01:56.216
Now what you see on the screen is a full Agent Loop

41
00:01:56.616 --> 00:02:00.520
with a START CONDITION, the Agent Loop itself, and a STOP CONDITION.

42
00:02:00.787 --> 00:02:04.591
To put it more definitively, the agent loop is a cyclical environment

43
00:02:04.791 --> 00:02:07.494
that executes for a specific amount of time

44
00:02:07.594 --> 00:02:09.963
where an LLM is involved and a response

45
00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:12.298
is observed and an action can be taken.

46
00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:15.535
It assembles, it invokes, and it acts

47
00:02:15.735 --> 00:02:17.737
until a stop condition is met.

48
00:02:18.238 --> 00:02:21.541
In pseudo code, the agent loop will look as shown on screen,

49
00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:24.544
where we have a MAX_STEPS specified,

50
00:02:24.744 --> 00:02:26.946
and we have an LLM that receives context,

51
00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:29.215
and we have an action that can be taken

52
00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:31.518
and then a final result that is extracted.

53
00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:35.021
Now we can go into memory operations

54
00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:37.223
in and out of the agent loop.

55
00:02:37.724 --> 00:02:40.026
Now that you understand what an agent loop is.

56
00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:43.029
Let's start with memory operations out of the agent loop.

57
00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:44.998
In previous lesson, we had an

58
00:02:44.998 --> 00:02:46.833
overview of different forms of agent memory.

59
00:02:46.833 --> 00:02:49.636
And we also had corresponding memory stores

60
00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:52.338
which are tables held in a database.

61
00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:56.342
These tables had operations held within a memory manager

62
00:02:56.543 --> 00:03:00.446
that exposed read and write operations that we can call upon.

63
00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.285
These operations are shown and utilized within the Agent Loop,

64
00:03:05.385 --> 00:03:08.288
specifically outside of the Agent Loop.

65
00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:11.291
We introduce a term harness because memory operations

66
00:03:11.391 --> 00:03:13.326
and operations in and out of the

67
00:03:13.326 --> 00:03:15.295
Agent Loop makes up the agent harness,

68
00:03:15.395 --> 00:03:18.498
which are programmatic scaffolding

69
00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:21.701
that enables the reliable execution of an AI agent.

70
00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:23.970
At a start condition, when we

71
00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.139
are about to enter the Agent Loop,

72
00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:30.243
we typically would have memory operations that read from memory.

73
00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:33.947
This is because we're assembling context for our LLMs.

74
00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:38.218
But we can also have memory operations that live and are executed

75
00:03:38.218 --> 00:03:40.420
within the agent loop itself.

76
00:03:40.954 --> 00:03:43.723
And this is because our agents can call on tools

77
00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:47.126
and we also have tools that are executed programmatically

78
00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:49.395
outside of the agent's control.

79
00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:53.700
Examples of these tools that can be executed by the agent

80
00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.369
within the Agent Loop are to search the internet,

81
00:03:56.369 --> 00:03:59.973
expand on a summary, or even summarize conversation.

82
00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.043
But summarize_conversation is an operation that can be executed

83
00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:06.846
within the Agent Loop programmatically

84
00:04:06.846 --> 00:04:09.415
without the agent having control over it.

85
00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:12.418
especially when we set a condition

86
00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:14.821
such as when the context window exceeds

87
00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:16.422
80% of its capacity.

88
00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:20.893
On the screen, you can see different operations that exist outside

89
00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:23.496
of the Agent Loop and inside of the Agent Loop.

90
00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:27.500
And as covered before, this makes up part of your harness

91
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:31.170
that makes a reliable memory-aware AI agent.

92
00:04:32.038 --> 00:04:33.773
In this lesson, we're going to

93
00:04:33.773 --> 00:04:35.742
put together everything we've been building up

94
00:04:35.842 --> 00:04:38.244
and build a Memory Aware Agent.

95
00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:41.848
We will start off by setting up our database.

96
00:04:42.415 --> 00:04:45.918
And then initializing a connection to the database

97
00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:48.655
represented by the database_connection variable.

98
00:04:48.655 --> 00:04:52.392
When you run the cell, you'll see a successful database setup

99
00:04:52.392 --> 00:04:56.963
as you've seen before. Next, we'll set up our OpenAI client

100
00:04:57.163 --> 00:05:00.266
and our embedding model as we've done before.

101
00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:02.669
Now, we'll set up our memory store,

102
00:05:02.769 --> 00:05:04.971
StoreManager, and MemoryManager.

103
00:05:05.171 --> 00:05:07.407
We'll start by specifying the names for

104
00:05:07.407 --> 00:05:09.976
all the memory tables as we've done before.

105
00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:14.180
Next, we'll create the conversational table and the tool log table

106
00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.282
just as we've done in previous lessons.

107
00:05:17.183 --> 00:05:19.085
Following on, we'll initialize

108
00:05:19.085 --> 00:05:21.387
our StoreManager by creating an instance

109
00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:24.190
of the StoreManager within this lesson.

110
00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:27.293
We would then also create data objects

111
00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:29.696
representing our memory tables

112
00:05:29.696 --> 00:05:31.264
from our databases.

113
00:05:31.864 --> 00:05:35.668
Finally, we will initialize our MemoryManager.

114
00:05:36.469 --> 00:05:39.706
Additionally, we will load up our toolbox

115
00:05:39.939 --> 00:05:43.743
and register some of the common tools we're going to use,

116
00:05:43.943 --> 00:05:45.878
such as the arXiv search and the

117
00:05:45.878 --> 00:05:48.047
tool that allows us to search for papers

118
00:05:48.147 --> 00:05:50.149
and save it into the database.

119
00:05:50.783 --> 00:05:53.586
When you execute the cell, you should see

120
00:05:53.786 --> 00:05:56.589
numbers of summary tools and common tools registered.

121
00:05:56.989 --> 00:05:59.592
These are tools that are available for your AI agent

122
00:05:59.692 --> 00:06:02.795
that is memory aware, and this will be agent triggered tools.

123
00:06:03.096 --> 00:06:05.898
In this part, we're going to load up

124
00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:09.202
all our context engineering techniques we explored in the last lesson.

125
00:06:09.602 --> 00:06:12.605
We've moved them into the helper file.

126
00:06:13.306 --> 00:06:16.876
Now that we have all the tools and the context

127
00:06:16.876 --> 00:06:20.980
engineering techniques loaded in this development environment for this lesson,

128
00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:24.984
we are going to start specifying our agent system prompt.

129
00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:28.454
This is the system prompt or system instruction

130
00:06:28.755 --> 00:06:31.758
that'll be passed into the LLM using the system role,

131
00:06:32.258 --> 00:06:33.860
which has a higher weight

132
00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.362
when the LLM looks at instruction provided

133
00:06:36.763 --> 00:06:38.731
within the system instruction.

134
00:06:38.998 --> 00:06:43.302
As you can see, this becomes even more memory aware

135
00:06:43.302 --> 00:06:46.839
as we tell the LLM the type of memory it has,

136
00:06:46.839 --> 00:06:49.008
how the context window is partitioned,

137
00:06:49.008 --> 00:06:54.008
and mention the different types of memory that we've created access to.

138
00:06:54.514 --> 00:06:57.617
This makes the LLM memory aware.

139
00:06:58.518 --> 00:07:01.020
Next, we have the function execute_tool,

140
00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:04.290
which allows us to call resources

141
00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:08.995
in the form of Python functions that exist within our development environment,

142
00:07:08.995 --> 00:07:12.298
which will allow us to call tools and execute functions

143
00:07:12.298 --> 00:07:17.003
by passing in the arguments provided by the LLM during tool call.

144
00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:20.339
And lastly, we have call_openai_chat,

145
00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:25.344
which just sends the context to an OpenAI model.

146
00:07:26.345 --> 00:07:29.348
Now, we can actually go into the agent loop.

147
00:07:29.649 --> 00:07:31.851
In the main agent loop,

148
00:07:31.851 --> 00:07:35.855
we're going to start by actually defining a function called call_agent

149
00:07:35.855 --> 00:07:38.157
that will take in the user query,

150
00:07:38.157 --> 00:07:42.862
which will be the start condition for actually triggering the agent loop.

151
00:07:42.862 --> 00:07:45.164
We'll specify a thread_id,

152
00:07:45.164 --> 00:07:47.667
as we want this to be session scoped,

153
00:07:47.667 --> 00:07:51.637
but also the max_iterations we want the agent loop to iterate for.

154
00:07:51.971 --> 00:07:55.775
In this slide, we've identified memory operations that occur

155
00:07:55.775 --> 00:07:59.178
outside of the agent loop and inside of the agent loop.

156
00:07:59.679 --> 00:08:01.747
We will start with memory operations

157
00:08:01.747 --> 00:08:04.183
that occur outside of the agent loop.

158
00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:07.253
And here, we can see memory operation

159
00:08:07.253 --> 00:08:09.856
that operates outside of the agent loop

160
00:08:09.856 --> 00:08:13.426
in where they're used to build the initial context.

161
00:08:13.426 --> 00:08:17.129
The steps we'll take are to build the conversational memory,

162
00:08:17.563 --> 00:08:19.599
the knowledge base, the workflow,

163
00:08:20.099 --> 00:08:22.068
entity, and summary memory.

164
00:08:22.768 --> 00:08:26.372
The next step is to have a deterministic

165
00:08:26.372 --> 00:08:28.774
memory operation executed.

166
00:08:29.175 --> 00:08:32.378
and that is to check if the context usage

167
00:08:32.378 --> 00:08:35.047
is exceeded or is equal to 80%.

168
00:08:35.481 --> 00:08:38.951
the usage of the context window is greater than 80%,

169
00:08:38.951 --> 00:08:42.255
we will trigger a summarization programmatically.

170
00:08:43.956 --> 00:08:47.260
Finally, we will have our entire context assigned

171
00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:49.262
to a variable context.

172
00:08:50.263 --> 00:08:52.465
The next step is to retrieve tools

173
00:08:52.465 --> 00:08:55.268
that are relevant for the query.

174
00:08:55.468 --> 00:08:58.971
Again, this read operation is a memory operation

175
00:08:58.971 --> 00:09:01.474
that is outside of the agent loop.

176
00:09:01.607 --> 00:09:03.509
This reduces the number of tools that

177
00:09:03.509 --> 00:09:05.411
we need to provide to the agent,

178
00:09:05.411 --> 00:09:09.115
as we will only require tools that are specific

179
00:09:09.115 --> 00:09:12.818
to the specific query being passed into the agent loop.

180
00:09:13.085 --> 00:09:15.888
Next, we'll write the conversational memory,

181
00:09:15.888 --> 00:09:18.658
the query, the role, and the thread_id.

182
00:09:19.358 --> 00:09:22.061
Now, before we get into the agent loop,

183
00:09:22.061 --> 00:09:25.064
the last step we do is create our message

184
00:09:25.064 --> 00:09:29.268
or the context we'll pass into the LLM within the agent loop.

185
00:09:29.669 --> 00:09:32.872
And this context is going to contain the AGENT_SYSTEM_PROMPT,

186
00:09:32.872 --> 00:09:35.675
which makes the LLM even more memory-aware.

187
00:09:36.142 --> 00:09:40.146
and the role and the context that we have to start with.

188
00:09:41.447 --> 00:09:44.150
In the agent loop, we use a call

189
00:09:44.216 --> 00:09:47.420
to the call_openai_chat function we explored earlier.

190
00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:50.222
We'll pass in the messages and the tools

191
00:09:50.222 --> 00:09:52.625
that are available for the agents to call upon.

192
00:09:53.225 --> 00:09:56.529
We will assign the response generated by the LLM

193
00:09:56.529 --> 00:09:58.130
to a variable called response.

194
00:09:58.497 --> 00:10:03.002
by analyzing and looking into the message within the response,

195
00:10:03.002 --> 00:10:07.006
we can decide if we're going to make a tool call operation

196
00:10:07.006 --> 00:10:10.276
or we're just going to move to a final response.

197
00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:12.912
Remember, in the agent loop,

198
00:10:12.912 --> 00:10:15.514
the agent can either decide to act

199
00:10:15.514 --> 00:10:17.516
or respond to the user,

200
00:10:17.516 --> 00:10:19.518
meeting a stop condition.

201
00:10:19.719 --> 00:10:22.221
If the agent decides to make a tool call,

202
00:10:22.221 --> 00:10:24.924
we will append this into the messages.

203
00:10:25.424 --> 00:10:28.427
Within the agent loop, we're able to execute tools

204
00:10:29.228 --> 00:10:31.330
and pass in the argument and the name

205
00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:36.335
that the agent has specified for execution. Finally,

206
00:10:36.369 --> 00:10:38.738
now within the agent loop, one of

207
00:10:38.738 --> 00:10:41.073
the memory operation we're going to conduct

208
00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:44.377
is to move some of the tool logs outside

209
00:10:44.377 --> 00:10:48.180
of the agent loop and into the database.

210
00:10:48.881 --> 00:10:50.483
This is context offloading.

211
00:10:50.783 --> 00:10:52.985
Once the tool result is received,

212
00:10:52.985 --> 00:10:55.688
the iteration of the agent loop will be incremented,

213
00:10:55.688 --> 00:10:58.991
and then we'll provide the LLM with the tool result.

214
00:10:58.991 --> 00:11:01.193
If the LLM decides to break,

215
00:11:02.028 --> 00:11:04.830
and actually go into a final answer,

216
00:11:05.531 --> 00:11:09.635
we would display this and assign it to the final_answer variable.

217
00:11:10.036 --> 00:11:11.437
There is also a situation where

218
00:11:11.437 --> 00:11:13.039
we step out of the agent loop,

219
00:11:13.239 --> 00:11:15.274
and that is when the max iteration

220
00:11:15.274 --> 00:11:17.343
has been reached without a final answer.

221
00:11:17.343 --> 00:11:21.947
and then we will give the final answer a pre-templated response

222
00:11:22.248 --> 00:11:24.050
in which we specify that the

223
00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:26.152
agent was unable to complete the request.

224
00:11:27.153 --> 00:11:30.356
Outside of the agent loop, we still have some memory operation.

225
00:11:30.856 --> 00:11:33.859
such as write_workflow and write_entity

226
00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:36.762
and write_conversational_memory.

227
00:11:37.163 --> 00:11:40.866
These are to capture some of the artifacts from the agent's execution,

228
00:11:41.267 --> 00:11:44.036
such as the number of steps that were

229
00:11:44.036 --> 00:11:47.139
taken to execute a query or achieve an objective,

230
00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:49.942
and we can store that as workflow memory

231
00:11:50.242 --> 00:11:53.446
and also the outcome of this specific step.

232
00:11:54.013 --> 00:11:57.783
We'll also write any entity that have been detected

233
00:11:57.783 --> 00:11:59.985
with the final_answer.

234
00:12:00.986 --> 00:12:03.989
And finally, we'll write_conversational_memory

235
00:12:04.223 --> 00:12:07.727
the final answer and the role of the assistant.

236
00:12:09.228 --> 00:12:11.430
This is an overview of the agent loop

237
00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:14.433
and the operations that happens in and out of the agent loop.

238
00:12:14.934 --> 00:12:18.437
Specifically, we have a memory-aware agent that we can call on

239
00:12:18.437 --> 00:12:21.040
and it can execute and perform operation

240
00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:23.242
with memory and continuity.

241
00:12:23.909 --> 00:12:25.911
Let's see this in action.

242
00:12:25.911 --> 00:12:28.214
To see our agent loop in action,

243
00:12:28.214 --> 00:12:30.516
we're just going to call the function call_agent

244
00:12:30.516 --> 00:12:31.884
and pass in a query, can

245
00:12:31.884 --> 00:12:33.486
you get me the paper on MemGPT

246
00:12:33.486 --> 00:12:37.857
with a unique thread ID. We're going to go for the output.

247
00:12:37.857 --> 00:12:39.859
As you can see, it's building the context

248
00:12:39.859 --> 00:12:42.561
and we can see the context window utilization.

249
00:12:43.562 --> 00:12:45.965
The LLM is aware of the question being asked,

250
00:12:45.965 --> 00:12:48.567
which is can you get me the paper MemGPT.

251
00:12:48.834 --> 00:12:51.337
And what we can see is different allocation

252
00:12:51.637 --> 00:12:54.640
of the context window to specific memory types.

253
00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:57.643
Conversation Memory, Knowledge Base Memory,

254
00:12:58.043 --> 00:13:01.947
Workflow Memory. Entity Memory and so on.

255
00:13:02.648 --> 00:13:04.650
For Conversation Memory,

256
00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:07.453
we can identify its conversation memory by this identifier

257
00:13:07.453 --> 00:13:09.955
using a markdown subheading.

258
00:13:10.456 --> 00:13:12.658
We're using markdown headings

259
00:13:12.658 --> 00:13:14.460
to enable the LLM to understand

260
00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:18.364
a hierarchical structure of information within a CONTEXT WINDOW.

261
00:13:19.064 --> 00:13:21.967
LLMs are able to understand this because this information

262
00:13:22.234 --> 00:13:25.271
or similar structured information are part of the training data.

263
00:13:25.471 --> 00:13:28.674
So they have a latent ability to understand structure

264
00:13:29.175 --> 00:13:32.478
and hierarchical information in markdown files.

265
00:13:33.145 --> 00:13:36.949
So within conversational memory, we specify how

266
00:13:37.049 --> 00:13:39.652
the agent should use this memory

267
00:13:39.752 --> 00:13:42.354
and when it should be leveraging it.

268
00:13:42.454 --> 00:13:45.357
This is making our LLM memory aware.

269
00:13:45.925 --> 00:13:49.228
We do this for every memory included in the context window,

270
00:13:49.728 --> 00:13:52.932
from knowledge base to entity memory and so on.

271
00:13:53.532 --> 00:13:56.135
For this specific run, because it's the first iteration

272
00:13:56.235 --> 00:13:59.238
of the agent loop, there are no retrieved messages.

273
00:13:59.505 --> 00:14:01.707
But because we've ingested some papers

274
00:14:01.707 --> 00:14:05.010
into our research agent earlier on,

275
00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:08.314
it will have knowledge of research papers.

276
00:14:08.314 --> 00:14:11.817
And this can be seen in its Knowledge Base Memory.

277
00:14:11.817 --> 00:14:15.020
The papers that are retrieved are ones

278
00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:17.623
that are semantically similar to the query passed in.

279
00:14:17.957 --> 00:14:20.459
In the first iteration of the AGENT LOOP,

280
00:14:20.459 --> 00:14:24.763
because the agent doesn't have any of the papers required

281
00:14:24.763 --> 00:14:27.566
to answer the question within its memory,

282
00:14:27.566 --> 00:14:30.569
it's using the tool arxiv_search_candidates.

283
00:14:31.337 --> 00:14:35.841
and is passing in a specific query, MemGPT, arXiv papers,

284
00:14:36.141 --> 00:14:39.144
and it's gotten a few response back.

285
00:14:39.645 --> 00:14:42.147
In the second iteration of the agent loop,

286
00:14:42.348 --> 00:14:45.618
the response from the tool call is returned to the agent.

287
00:14:45.618 --> 00:14:49.421
or to the LLM, and then it provides the final ANSWER

288
00:14:49.622 --> 00:14:51.924
where it can then exit the AGENT LOOP.

289
00:14:52.424 --> 00:14:55.527
So in the second iteration, we see our ANSWER.

290
00:14:56.228 --> 00:14:59.098
It provides us with the MemGPT paper

291
00:14:59.598 --> 00:15:02.201
and an overview of information allocated to it.

292
00:15:02.601 --> 00:15:06.105
And also specifies the next actions it recommends to take.

293
00:15:06.572 --> 00:15:10.376
To showcase the continuity of our agent,

294
00:15:10.376 --> 00:15:13.045
we will continue the conversation

295
00:15:13.045 --> 00:15:15.347
by calling the call_agent function again

296
00:15:15.347 --> 00:15:18.050
and ask it, can you save the content of the paper?

297
00:15:18.584 --> 00:15:20.886
This is to showcase the Conversational Memory

298
00:15:20.986 --> 00:15:23.188
because the agent can carry on the conversation

299
00:15:23.289 --> 00:15:25.958
and has an awareness of the paper we're referring to,

300
00:15:26.058 --> 00:15:28.360
which should be MemGPT. As we can

301
00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:30.663
see, the CONTEXT WINDOW is being built.

302
00:15:31.096 --> 00:15:33.899
And in Conversation Memory, we can see the previous answer

303
00:15:33.899 --> 00:15:37.603
or previous interaction that we had.

304
00:15:37.603 --> 00:15:39.405
The first question we provided

305
00:15:39.405 --> 00:15:41.206
alongside the response.

306
00:15:41.707 --> 00:15:44.810
We've asked the agent to save the paper,

307
00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:48.314
and we expect it to conduct tool calls to do this.

308
00:15:48.881 --> 00:15:53.185
But first, we can see that the Workflow Memory is being filled

309
00:15:53.285 --> 00:15:55.788
with some information from the previous step.

310
00:15:56.088 --> 00:15:58.657
which you can see with the query was

311
00:15:58.657 --> 00:16:00.259
can you get me the paper MemGPT

312
00:16:00.559 --> 00:16:04.063
and the steps taken has been recorded with the outcomes.

313
00:16:05.064 --> 00:16:07.866
In this step, the agent has access to tools

314
00:16:08.067 --> 00:16:10.169
and is going to use the tool

315
00:16:10.169 --> 00:16:12.271
fetch and save paper to knowledge base

316
00:16:12.271 --> 00:16:13.872
that we specified earlier.

317
00:16:14.206 --> 00:16:17.009
is able to get the right paper

318
00:16:17.009 --> 00:16:20.412
from the previous conversation because it has conversational memory.

319
00:16:20.913 --> 00:16:24.316
and in the second iteration of this, it provides an answer

320
00:16:24.316 --> 00:16:26.986
where it says it saved the paper for us.

321
00:16:27.619 --> 00:16:30.422
To showcase further continuity,

322
00:16:30.422 --> 00:16:32.324
we will ask the agent to pull

323
00:16:32.324 --> 00:16:34.226
out some main takeaways from the paper.

324
00:16:35.127 --> 00:16:37.796
Again, we don't have to specify the paper we're referring to

325
00:16:37.796 --> 00:16:40.799
because all information is within the conversational memory.

326
00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:43.602
Now let's see the response from the agent

327
00:16:43.602 --> 00:16:45.371
in terms of getting a response

328
00:16:45.371 --> 00:16:47.406
to the main takeaways from the paper.

329
00:16:47.773 --> 00:16:49.208
One thing to note is the

330
00:16:49.208 --> 00:16:50.876
response of the agent is being conducted

331
00:16:50.876 --> 00:16:52.778
with one iteration of the AGENT LOOP, which means

332
00:16:52.778 --> 00:16:54.880
we don't have to go look for the paper again,

333
00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:57.783
and we could just look into the conversational history

334
00:16:57.783 --> 00:17:01.687
and other memory segmentation of the CONTEXT WINDOW

335
00:17:01.687 --> 00:17:03.589
to actually provide an answer.

336
00:17:04.523 --> 00:17:07.526
And the answer is provided in a structured format.

337
00:17:08.394 --> 00:17:10.496
Here you can see we have a memory-aware agent

338
00:17:10.696 --> 00:17:12.698
that is able to perform different tasks

339
00:17:12.998 --> 00:17:15.200
and is aware of conversational memory

340
00:17:15.401 --> 00:17:18.003
and can utilize other memory partitions

341
00:17:18.003 --> 00:17:20.806
such as the workflow memory to store previous steps.

342
00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:23.442
To see summary memory in action,

343
00:17:23.442 --> 00:17:25.577
we're going to ask the agent

344
00:17:25.577 --> 00:17:28.047
to summarize the conversation using its tool.

345
00:17:28.747 --> 00:17:32.451
The ability to summarize conversation is both a deterministic

346
00:17:32.451 --> 00:17:35.254
and an agent-triggered memory operation.

347
00:17:35.754 --> 00:17:38.757
So, that means that the agent at its discretion

348
00:17:38.757 --> 00:17:41.360
could summarize the conversation if the context window

349
00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:43.462
is getting to a capacity

350
00:17:43.462 --> 00:17:45.564
it feels it needs for it to summarize.

351
00:17:45.831 --> 00:17:48.667
by calling the summarization context tool,

352
00:17:48.667 --> 00:17:51.036
which will see an entry into our summary memory.

353
00:17:51.336 --> 00:17:53.539
But first, we will see the tool being used,

354
00:17:53.639 --> 00:17:55.941
then in another iteration of the call_agent,

355
00:17:55.941 --> 00:17:58.143
we'll be able to see that the

356
00:17:58.143 --> 00:18:00.345
summary memory has a placeholder in it.

357
00:18:00.779 --> 00:18:03.582
and the Conversation Memory has been reduced.

358
00:18:04.850 --> 00:18:06.952
Looking at the tool calls, we can see

359
00:18:06.952 --> 00:18:08.954
that the agent has summarized the memory

360
00:18:08.954 --> 00:18:11.290
by utilizing the summarize_and_store tool.

361
00:18:11.824 --> 00:18:15.727
As we can see, it's marked seven messages as summarized

362
00:18:15.961 --> 00:18:20.699
and also created a Summary ID in our summary memory store.

363
00:18:21.533 --> 00:18:24.069
In the next iteration, we should be able

364
00:18:24.069 --> 00:18:26.638
to see that a conversational history is less

365
00:18:26.839 --> 00:18:29.341
and we have an entry in our summary memory

366
00:18:29.541 --> 00:18:32.311
where right now there are no available summaries.

367
00:18:32.644 --> 00:18:34.746
Let's observe this.

368
00:18:34.746 --> 00:18:38.250
We're going to ask the agent what our first question was,

369
00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:40.752
which should not exist in the conversational memory

370
00:18:40.752 --> 00:18:42.754
as we have summarized the conversation.

371
00:18:43.155 --> 00:18:45.257
As we can see, the retrieve messages

372
00:18:45.257 --> 00:18:47.960
of the Conversation Memory only contains

373
00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:51.964
a response from the assistant that says it stored a summary.

374
00:18:51.964 --> 00:18:54.967
But the previous interactions did not exist.

375
00:18:55.801 --> 00:18:59.605
In the summary memory segment of the context window,

376
00:18:59.605 --> 00:19:03.609
we can see that there is a placeholder for a summary ID

377
00:19:03.642 --> 00:19:05.811
and a description of what the

378
00:19:05.811 --> 00:19:08.313
summary contains, which was a MemGPT paper

379
00:19:08.347 --> 00:19:12.651
that was stored in SEMANTIC_MEMORY and a key insight summarized.

380
00:19:12.985 --> 00:19:16.522
Another thing to point out is Entity Memory here in action.

381
00:19:17.222 --> 00:19:19.391
In Entity Memory, we're trying to capture

382
00:19:19.892 --> 00:19:23.595
entity information such as people, places and organization,

383
00:19:23.896 --> 00:19:25.898
and you can see from our interaction,

384
00:19:25.998 --> 00:19:29.201
we have captured those in several different instances,

385
00:19:29.501 --> 00:19:31.303
such as the author of the paper.

386
00:19:31.637 --> 00:19:34.640
and other information. But remember,

387
00:19:34.740 --> 00:19:38.143
we asked the agent what our first question was.

388
00:19:38.243 --> 00:19:40.846
But in order to answer what our first question was,

389
00:19:40.946 --> 00:19:43.148
it would have to unpack a summary.

390
00:19:43.182 --> 00:19:45.284
And we can see it does this

391
00:19:45.284 --> 00:19:47.686
as within the iteration of the AGENT LOOP,

392
00:19:47.986 --> 00:19:50.322
it expands a summary of the ID

393
00:19:50.322 --> 00:19:52.691
that it has in its summary memory.

394
00:19:53.192 --> 00:19:58.163
And now it has information as to the actual summary

395
00:19:58.664 --> 00:20:02.167
or the uncompressed form of the summary that was generated.

396
00:20:02.601 --> 00:20:05.137
And finally, it can answer the question

397
00:20:05.137 --> 00:20:07.206
that our first question was,

398
00:20:07.506 --> 00:20:09.608
can you get me the paper MemGPT

399
00:20:10.108 --> 00:20:11.810
and the time it was sent.

400
00:20:12.611 --> 00:20:14.713
You've seen summary memory in action,

401
00:20:15.514 --> 00:20:18.016
context offloading and context retrieval,

402
00:20:18.417 --> 00:20:21.420
all implemented with memory engineering techniques

403
00:20:21.520 --> 00:20:25.424
within an agent loop and having a memory-aware agent.

404
00:20:26.191 --> 00:20:29.094
You've come to an end of lesson 5.

405
00:20:29.294 --> 00:20:32.297
In this lesson, we've built a memory-aware agent.

406
00:20:32.397 --> 00:20:34.366
Well done.
